BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2341@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T085117Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T152000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T154000
SUMMARY:RISC-V Svnapot: dynamic fold/unfold for folded PTEs
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:RISC-V MC · Yunhui Cui · https://lpc.events/event/20/contribu
 tions/2341/\n\nSvnapot gives RISC-V an architectural way to encode contigu
 ous PTE ranges as folded mappings\, and 64K mTHP is a particularly good ma
 tch\nfor that capability.\n\nThis session presents a patch series that ena
 bles that model through dynamic fold/unfold and a split between public and
  raw page-table\nAPIs. Public helpers preserve the logical sub-PTE view ex
 pected by core MM\, while raw helpers remain available to architecture-pri
 vate\nusers that need direct access to the encoded hardware entry. In our 
 measurements\, combining 64K mTHP with Svnapot aggregation reduces\nlat_me
 m_rd latency by about 12% compared with non-aggregated 4K mappings\, impro
 ves overall SPEC CPU performance by around 2%\, and\ndelivers about 7% on 
 the instruction-TLB-sensitive 520.omnetpp_r sub-benchmark.\n\nThe session 
 will also discuss whether fold/unfold transitions can avoid intermediate T
 LB flushes\, and examine the trade-offs between\nhardware 64K pages and Sv
 napot-based aggregation\, including the advantages and drawbacks of each a
 nd whether Svnapot should support\nadditional aggregation granularities be
 yond today’s 64K mTHP use case.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2341/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2455@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T085117Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T154000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T160000
SUMMARY:Optimizing the kernel with vector
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:RISC-V MC · Tao Chiu · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributi
 ons/2455/\n\nThe territory of using a larger context in the kernel-mode ha
 s been a forbidden topic since the existence of Linux. The use of SIMD uni
 t in the kernel-mode is strictly restricted because the program is highly 
 optimized for stack footprint\, responsiveness\, and maximum hardware comp
 atibility. However\, as hardware and compiler technologies advance\, the b
 enefit of enabling autovectorization has become more viable\, and it might
  be a good time to revisit it.\n\nIn this talk we will go through some ini
 tial measurements to see the benefit of compiling the risc-v kernel with a
 utovectorization\, challenges of managing VLA context and ways to maintain
  code when compiled with such feature.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2455/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2626@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T085117Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T160000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T162000
SUMMARY:RVA23 in practice: discovery\, dispatch\, and the hardware left beh
 ind
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:RISC-V MC · Guodong Xu · https://lpc.events/event/20/contribu
 tions/2626/\n\nRVA23 is ratified and the first (vendor-announced) complian
 t silicon is shipping\, but between "the profile exists" and "userspace ca
 n rely on it" sit three unresolved kernel questions.\n\n**Discovery.** Ext
 ension capability discovery between user mode and the kernel is still mess
 y: hwprobe and prctl can disagree\, so which interface should IFUNC resolv
 ers actually trust\, or does the discovery contract itself need a rethink?
  The "riscv: hwprobe: export the availability of vector to user" series un
 der discussion on linux-riscv is a live example.\n\n**Dispatch.** (runtime
  selection of optimized code paths) x86 already does coarse\, library-leve
 l selection via glibc-hwcaps. Could the same mechanism sit on top of hwpro
 be on RISC-V\, keyed on something like rva23u64? Is profile granularity ev
 en the right key? For distros like Debian that will not flip their baselin
 e any time soon\, this is probably the realistic path. But there is a catc
 h though\, as the prctl/hwprobe mismatch shows.\n\n**The hardware left beh
 ind.** What compatibility fallbacks do pre-RVA23 systems get? And further 
 out: can kernel-side cooperation support RVA23 capability certification\, 
 and what test infrastructure would that take?\n\nAiming at concrete next s
 teps for the discovery contract and a dispatch story distros can adopt. Fo
 ur asks to the room:\n\n**Ask 1**  kernel maintainers: is the RVA23U64 bit
  the right shape?\n**Ask 2**  distros: ship rva23u64 builds of selected li
 braries?\n**Ask 3**  kernel maintainers: deprecate the V prctl?\n**Ask 4**
   future extensions: always-on\, or dynamically enabled per process?
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2626/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2454@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T085117Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T162000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T163000
SUMMARY:Extensions - What's new? What's coming up?
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:RISC-V MC · Tom Gall · https://lpc.events/event/20/contributi
 ons/2454/\n\nThe RISC-V ISA specs contained the ratified collection of bas
 es\, extensions and profiles. This talk will cover recently ratified exten
 sions\, as well as cover upcoming extensions and profiles which are close 
 to ratification. We'll dive into kernel / user space design considerations
  that implementors will want to care about.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2454/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2342@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T085117Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T170000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T171500
SUMMARY:Local\, SBI\, or IPI? Tracing RISC-V TLB Flush Decisions in Linux
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:RISC-V MC · Roman Storozhenko · https://lpc.events/event/20/c
 ontributions/2342/\n\nRISC-V Linux TLB flushing has several runtime paths.
  A flush may be local to the current hart\, may use SBI remote fence suppo
 rt\, or may fall back to Linux IPIs. The kernel also makes range-versus-fu
 ll decisions\, performs ASID-aware flushing\, and carries different flush 
 strides for normal and huge-page ranges. These decisions can matter when d
 ebugging correctness issues\, performance anomalies\, and SMP scalability 
 problems\, but they are not currently visible in a compact RISC-V-specific
  form.\n\nThis talk presents a small RFC tracepoint patch that makes the L
 inux-side RISC-V TLB flush decision visible. The proposed tracepoint recor
 ds the requested flush range\, stride\, ASID\, target CPU count\, flush ty
 pe\, threshold decision\, and selected transport path: local\, SBI remote 
 fence\, or IPI fallback.\n\nThe goal is intentionally narrow. This is not 
 a hardware TLB profiler and not a complete MM observability framework. It 
 focuses on one practical question: when Linux decides to invalidate RISC-V
  address translations\, can a developer easily see which path was selected
  and why?\n\nThe talk will walk through the relevant RISC-V kernel TLB-flu
 sh path\, explain what existing tracing can and cannot show\, and demonstr
 ate the proposed tracepoint using QEMU and one real RISC-V board. A small 
 multi-threaded mmap() / mprotect() / munmap() workload will be used to tri
 gger local and remote flushes and to show range-size effects around the cu
 rrent threshold logic.\n\nAttendees will leave with a concrete understandi
 ng of how RISC-V TLB flush decisions are made in Linux\, how they can be o
 bserved today\, what a minimal tracepoint adds\, and what trade-offs shoul
 d be considered before adding architecture-specific observability to hot M
 M paths.\n\nComments:\n\nThis topic is primarily intended for the RISC-V M
 C because it focuses on arch/riscv MM/TLB behavior\, including local flush
 es\, SBI remote fences\, and IPI fallback paths. If the organizers think t
 he tracing or MM angle is a better fit elsewhere\, it could also be consid
 ered for the Tracing MC or Kernel Memory Management MC.\n\nI previously pr
 esented in the FOSDEM 2026 Kernel devroom with a practical talk on reprodu
 cing a Linux kernel bug using virtme-ng. This LPC proposal is a different 
 topic\, but it follows the same practical style: a small kernel RFC patch\
 , reproducible QEMU testing\, and validation on real RISC-V hardware where
  possible.\n\nP.S. Patches to review:\nv1\nhttps://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20
 260829-tlb_tracepoint-v1-1-dfdaede7e741@gmail.com/T/#me0739340db5d4dfc6e0f
 f1675896129af941eca5\nv2\nhttps://lore.kernel.org/linux-riscv/20260830-tlb
 _tracepoint-v2-1-e3c88c24df5b@gmail.com/T/#u\nv2 re-send:\nhttps://lore.ke
 rnel.org/lkml/20260924-tlb_tracepoint-v2-1-4e3e78ef5cdb@gmail.com/\nasciin
 ema presentation:\nhttps://asciinema.org/a/1266683
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2342/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2414@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T085117Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T171500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T173000
SUMMARY:Error INJection support for RAS on RISC-V architecture
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:RISC-V MC · Himanshu Chauhan · https://lpc.events/event/20/co
 ntributions/2414/\n\nError INJection (EINJ)\, defined by ACPI\, offers a p
 latform-independent mechanism to inject hardware errors and validate syste
 m resilience paths. By avoiding platform-specific tooling\, EINJ enables c
 onsistent testing of the Linux error-handling stack across architectures\,
  including APEI and broader RAS workflows.\n\nThis session discuss the des
 ign and implementation strategy for enabling EINJ on RISC-V platforms. It 
 will cover architecture-specific considerations\, firmware and kernel inte
 gration points\, and practical validation flows for injected error scenari
 os. The goal is to establish a robust\, upstream-friendly approach that br
 ings RISC-V closer to feature parity with existing ACPI-based RAS ecosyste
 ms and improves confidence in production reliability.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2414/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2422@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T085117Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T173000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T174500
SUMMARY:RISC-V Trace support in ACPI
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:RISC-V MC · SUNIL V L · https://lpc.events/event/20/contribut
 ions/2422/\n\nTo support RISC-V E-trace/N-trace components in ACPI\, we pr
 opose using the Device Graph UUID defined in the DSD Guide\, aligned with 
 how similar topologies are described on other architectures. Beyond discus
 sing the proposal itself and how it should appear in the ACPI namespace\, 
 we also want feedback on implementation strategy.\n\n  Today\, ACPI fwnode
  graph handling does not interpret Device Graph UUID data\, so trace drive
 rs parse ACPI graph links in-driver. We have a PoC with that approach\, bu
 t it duplicates logic\, raises long-term maintenance cost\, and keeps DT a
 nd ACPI paths separate instead of reusing common fwnode_graph_* helpers.\n
 \n  Our proposal is to extend ACPI fwnode graph support\, so Device Graph 
 UUID data is translated into standard fwnode_graph_* operations. This allo
 ws shared trace-driver code across DT and ACPI\, reduces duplication\, and
  scales better as additional architectures adopt this representation.\n\n 
  We plan to post an RFC series before the conference so maintainers and su
 bsystem stakeholders can review concrete patches and align on direction.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2422/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2478@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T085117Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T174500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T180500
SUMMARY:One Kernel Per Cluster: Multikernel for Heterogeneous RISC-V
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:RISC-V MC · Cong Wang\, Yuning Liang · https://lpc.events/eve
 nt/20/contributions/2478/\n\nBoot mainline on SpacemiT's Key Stone K3 and 
 half the machine stays dark: eight 1024-bit AI cores rejected by smpboot b
 ecause riscv_v_vsize allows exactly one vlenb per system. This is not a br
 ing-up bug\, it is the first shipping proof that RISC-V vendors will not p
 ay for matched vector widths\, and the SG2042's RVV 0.7.1 installed base m
 akes it worse: not narrower\, incompatible. Per-process vlenb patches can 
 boot this machine\, but no patch changes the physics: 4 KB of vector state
  cannot land in a 1 KB register file\, so any single-kernel fix confines v
 ector tasks to one cluster for life and inherits the arm64 asymmetric-32bi
 t price list: no hotplug of the last capable core\, no SCHED_DEADLINE admi
 ssion\, silently rewritten affinity masks\, undefined cpuset isolation\, p
 lus hwprobe intersecting capabilities down to no vector unit anywhere on a
  0.7.1/1.0 mix. The partition was decided at tape-out\; the scheduler can 
 only hide it\, expensively. We propose making it first class instead: mult
 ikernel boots one independent Linux kernel per cluster\, each compiled for
  its exact silicon\, seeing a machine where every core matches. The capabi
 lity word tells the truth\, glibc picks real vector routines\, hotplug and
  deadline scheduling and cpusets just work\, and RVV 0.7.1 and 1.0 domains
  coexist on one board. The prototype exists (github.com/multikernel/linux)
 \; what it needs is this room. We will present the architecture and the RI
 SC-V gaps we want to close with the community: kernel spawning on the RISC
 -V boot flow\, cross-domain interrupt routing with AIA/IMSIC\, and DT/ACPI
  bindings for heterogeneous vector topology. Heterogeneous VLEN is the RIS
 C-V roadmap\, not a corner case. Let's give it a first-class answer before
  the next SoC tapes out.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2478/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2479@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261006T085117Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T180500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T182000
SUMMARY:Supporting Heterogeneous VLEN on RISC-V: Lessons from SpacemiT K3
LOCATION:Small Theatre\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:RISC-V MC · Qiubin Zhuang\, Dong Xu · https://lpc.events/even
 t/20/contributions/2479/\n\nSpacemiT K3 is among the first production RVA2
 3-compatible SoCs\, shipping\, features heterogeneous vector lengths:\n\n-
  **8× X100 cores**: RV64GCV\, VLEN=256\, full RVA23 compliance\, H-extens
 ion — general-purpose\n- **8× A100 cores**: RV64GCV\, VLEN=1024\, no H-
 extension — AI/vector compute\n\nBoth core types implement RVV 1.0\, but
  their incompatible vector register widths create a correctness issue: if 
 a thread using vector instructions migrates between core types\, its vecto
 r state corrupts. This isn't a performance concern — it's a data integri
 ty problem.\n\nOur current vendor-specific approach (CONFIG_SPACEMIT_HMP) 
 enforces strict per-thread core-type binding. This works\, but it's not up
 stream-friendly.\n\n---\n\n## What SpacemiT Can Contribute\n\nWe can provi
 de engineering data from 2+ years of HMP scheduling in production\, includ
 ing details on vector state management\, signal frames\, ptrace\, and CPU 
 hotplug. We'll also share real constraints — why certain approaches work
  or don't on actual silicon.\n\nK3 development boards are available for te
 sting\, and we have deployment experience with 9+ distributions (Ubuntu\, 
 Debian\, Fedora\, openEuler\, openKylin\, etc.). We're committed to upstre
 am patches based on community consensus.\n\n---\n\n## Proposed Discussion 
 Topics\n\n### 1. Kernel mechanism options\n\nWe've explored several approa
 ches and can share trade-offs:\n- Generic heterogeneous-VLEN scheduling (s
 imilar to ARM capacity-aware)\n- Cpuset/cgroup-based isolation\n- ISA-capa
 bility-aware scheduling\n- Userspace-only manual pinning\n\n### 2. Vector 
 state management\n\n- Should `riscv_v_vsize` be per-task or per-CPU?\n- Si
 gnal frame handling with variable VLEN?\n- ptrace behavior?\n\n### 3. DT b
 indings and discoverability\n\n- Should VLEN capabilities be explicit in D
 T or runtime-discovered?\n- Standardization of per-core capability propert
 ies?\n\n### 4. RVA23 ecosystem assumptions\n\n- Should RVA23 distributions
  assume uniform VLEN=256?\n- How should applications/libraries handle VLEN
  variability?\n- Kernel enforcement vs. userspace detection?\n\n### 5. Sho
 uld kernel support this at all?\n\n- Is this a legitimate use case (like A
 RM big.LITTLE) or a hardware constraint software shouldn't accommodate?\n\
 n---\n\n## Session Format\n\nWe suggest a community-driven discussion. Spa
 cemiT can provide engineering data and K3-specific details\, while communi
 ty maintainers lead the architectural direction. We'll share detailed tech
 nical background (2-3 pages) before the conference.\n\n---\n\n## Why This 
 Matters\n\n- **RISC-V flexibility vs. portability**: vendors can customize
  per-core ISA\, but software must remain portable\n- **This is solvable**:
  ARM addressed big.LITTLE heterogeneity\; RISC-V can too\n- **Prevent frag
 mentation**: if every vendor ships custom scheduling\, distro/kernel maint
 enance becomes unsustainable\n\n---\n\n## Desired Outcomes\n\n1. Community
  consensus on whether/how to support heterogeneous VLEN upstream\n2. DT bi
 nding direction for representing per-core capabilities\n3. Clear action it
 ems for follow-up patches and testing\n\n---\n\n## Pre-Conference Material
 s\n\nWe will circulate 2-3 pages of technical background (HMP implementati
 on\, trade-offs\, deployment observations\, code pointers) to participants
  before the conference.\n\nReference: https://github.com/spacemit-com/linu
 x
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2479/
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
